      "math_academy_id": "q-71577",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}{{e}^{x-y}},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}{{e}^{x-y}} & =-2 \\\\ z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-2$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}.$",
